---
title: "Auto-Generate Columns"
enterprise: true
---

Auto-Generate Columns creates column definitions from `rowData`, so you don't need to define them upfront. This is useful when working with dynamic or unknown data shapes.

Set `autoGenerateColumnDefs` to `true`. The grid reads the keys from the first row of `rowData` and creates a column for each one. No `columnDefs` are needed:

{% gridExampleRunner title="Auto-Generate Columns" name="nested-objects" exampleHeight=300 /%}

```js
const gridOptions = {
    autoGenerateColumnDefs: true,
    rowData: myData,
};
```

## Column Generation

The grid inspects the first row of `rowData` and creates columns based on the value types it finds:

- **Primitive values** (strings, numbers, booleans, bigints, Date instances) produce a leaf column.
- **Plain objects** produce a column group, recursing into nested keys. The `headerName` for each column is derived from its key name.
- **Arrays** and **null/undefined** values produce a leaf column by default.
- **Functions** are skipped.

For example, this row data:

```js
rowData: [
    { user: { name: 'Alice', age: 30 }, score: 100 },
]
```

Produces:

```js
columnDefs: [
    {
        headerName: 'User',
        children: [
            { field: 'user.name', headerName: 'Name' },
            { field: 'user.age', headerName: 'Age' },
        ],
    },
    { field: 'score' },
]
```

## Configuration

Pass an `AutoGenerateColumnDefsConfig` object instead of `true` to control how different value types are handled:

```js
const gridOptions = {
    autoGenerateColumnDefs: {
        objectValues: 'recurse', // 'recurse' (default) | 'skip' | 'flat'
        arrayValues: 'include',  // 'include' (default) | 'skip'
        nullValues: 'include',   // 'include' (default) | 'skip'
    },
    rowData: myData,
};
```

| Option         | Values                          | Default     | Description                                                                                                                  |
| -------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `objectValues` | `'recurse'`, `'skip'`, `'flat'` | `'recurse'` | `'recurse'` creates column groups with dot-notation fields. `'flat'` creates a single leaf column. `'skip'` ignores the key. |
| `arrayValues`  | `'include'`, `'skip'`           | `'include'` | `'include'` creates a leaf column. `'skip'` ignores the key.                                                                 |
| `nullValues`   | `'include'`, `'skip'`           | `'include'` | `'include'` creates a leaf column for `null` or `undefined` values. `'skip'` ignores the key.                                |

## Updating Row Data

Columns are regenerated each time `rowData` is set. Only the keys from the first row are used, so if the new data has a different shape, columns change accordingly.

By default, column order matches the key order of the first row. Set `maintainColumnOrder` to `true` to preserve existing column positions when the data shape changes:

```js
const gridOptions = {
    autoGenerateColumnDefs: true,
    maintainColumnOrder: true,
};
```

See [Maintain Column Order](./column-updating-definitions/#maintain-column-order) for details.

{% note %}
Transactions (`applyTransaction` / `applyTransactionAsync`) do not trigger column regeneration because they update rows without replacing `rowData`. To regenerate columns after a transaction, set `rowData` directly.
{% /note %}

## Customising Generated Columns

Use `processAutoGeneratedColumnDefs` to modify, reorder, or replace the generated columns before they are applied:

```js
const gridOptions = {
    autoGenerateColumnDefs: true,
    processAutoGeneratedColumnDefs: (params) => {
        return params.colDefs.map((colDef) => ({
            ...colDef,
            sortable: true,
            filter: true,
        }));
    },
};
```

The callback receives `params.colDefs` (the generated definitions, which may include `ColGroupDef` entries for nested objects) and `params.rowData`. The returned array is used as the final `columnDefs`.

## Clearing Data

Setting `rowData` to `[]` or `null` clears rows but preserves column definitions. To reset columns as well:

```js
api.setGridOption('columnDefs', []);
```

